    "      \"limits\": [\n",
    "        \"Source support and qualification are stated separately. Supported means the entrypoint exists in the host source, which is not parity. Executed evidence is stated separately and names the actual profile, operation, artifact, build and platform; it is never inferred for another host, and browser, whole-Core, general-ingestion and publication claims are not made here.\",\n",
    "        \"Inspect is not executable admission. It reads and reports a closed native-produced model view and runs nothing; produce, predict, calibrate and retrain are admitted by the owners (Core, DAG-ML, IO, Methods) when each is requested, and a refusal is never replaced by a fallback.\",\n",
    "        \"Training scope: for an admitted numeric IO-backed record that mixes train and test rows, the complete original record is stored byte-identically, while the training universe, relations, envelope and FoldSet hold only the train IDs. Declared fold indices are relative to the train block in original IO order. CV, OOF selection, final refit and preprocessing exclude held-out rows, which serve stored and external prediction only and are not scored or used for selection automatically. All-train behavior is unchanged. This claims no other family, platform, operation or transport run_pipeline support.\",\n",
    "        \"Raw canonical archive bytes and owner metadata remain authoritative. Hosts pass bytes and strings through, and do not parse a record, recipe or package, hash scientific content, or train and replay outside the owners.\",\n",
    "        \"The profile id and version, the container schema nirs4all.n4a.container.v1, the Methods ABI and wire format, the recipe language and the package releases are independent compatibility dimensions; none implies another.\",\n",
    "        \"Calibration is declared on the Rust surface only (calibrate_n4a_native, which keeps the native target-index and calibration behavior). JavaScript/WASM has no calibration, and the bounded WASM scope does not narrow native behavior. Python and MATLAB/Octave declare no canonical calibration; their existing calibrate functions belong to native-conformal and Archive V2.\",\n",
    "        \"Native actions keep record and model state in memory; Core charges its own copies to the declared working-memory budget, and allocations inside IO, DAG and Methods are outside it.\",\n",
